    "available_at": "2026-10-14T21:23:17.822892+00:00",
    "body": "Venti Technologies plans to introduce driverless container -moving trucks at a U.S. intermodal rail yard in 2026, beginning a rollout that could put more than 130 autonomous vehicles at eight railroad sites by 2027. The Boston-based company announced the planned launch Oct. 1 but has not named the railroad or the first location.\nWhat’s Related\nThe trucks will move shipping containers inside operating rail yards, where freight transfers between trucks and trains. Venti says its vehicles can work alongside cranes, forklifts, trains, and human-driven trucks without dedicated lanes or changes to the terminal.\nThe rollout follows a long-term commercial agreement announced in July with an unnamed Class I North American railroad. That agreement includes potential expansion to more than 600 vehicles by the end of the decade, although Venti has not disclosed how many trucks will initially enter service.\nVenti describes the planned deployment as North America’s first autonomous truck fleet for an intermodal rail yard. The company said it would provide more details in the coming weeks, but its announcement did not include an exact launch date.\nTrucks operating through a busy yard\nThe planned trucks will operate without drivers on board, using Venti’s software to navigate through live yard traffic. The company says its vehicles handle positioning and driving decisions on board and can operate day and night, including in rain, snow and other bad weather.\nCustomers can use newly produced trucks or retrofit existing vehicles with Venti’s technology .\nAlthough Venti describes the trucks as loading, moving, and unloading containers, it did not explain which loading tasks the vehicles perform or how they coordinate with container-handling equipment.\nExperience in Singapore\nVenti points to its work at PSA Singapore as evidence that its technology can handle busy freight operations. The company says its autonomous trucks have operated around the clock in full commercial production there for nearly three years, working among 1,200 human-driven trucks.\nAccording to Venti, its vehicles have moved more than 360,000 containers in Singapore. The company also reports more than 500,000 autonomous miles across its fleet with no critical incidents, although the announcement did not define that term or provide an independent safety assessment.",
